Git is a version control system used to track changes in your code.

Simple example

You create a project:

My AI Chatbot

You make changes every day:

Day 1 → Create project
Day 2 → Add FastAPI
Day 3 → Add chatbot
Day 4 → Fix bugs
Day 5 → Deploy

Git keeps track of these changes, like a history or save points in a game.

======

Basic Git workflow
Your Code
   ↓
git add .
   ↓
git commit -m "Added chatbot feature"
   ↓
git push
   ↓
GitHub Repository

====

Important commands

| Command                   | Meaning                             |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| `git init`                | Start Git in a project              |
| `git status`              | Check changed files                 |
| `git add .`               | Select all changes                  |
| `git commit -m "message"` | Save a version                      |
| `git log`                 | View history                        |
| `git push`                | Upload code to GitHub               |
| `git pull`                | Download latest changes from GitHub |
| `git clone`               | Copy a GitHub project               |


======

Git vs GitHub
Git = Tracks your code changes on your computer.
GitHub = Online platform where you store and share Git repositories.

====

Your CI/CD connection
You write code
      ↓
Git tracks changes
      ↓
git push
      ↓
GitHub
      ↓
GitHub Actions (CI/CD)
      ↓
Test + Build
      ↓
Deploy to Cloud Run 🚀

So, Git is the tool that manages your code history, and GitHub can trigger CI/CD when you push your updated code.

Git records your code history locally, while GitHub stores the repository online and can trigger automation when you 
push a committed change to GitHub.

